<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mary Magdalene</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mary of Magdala, the Magdalene</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mary Magdalene was, according to the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">canonical gospels</a>, a woman who travelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> as one of his followers and witnessed his crucifixion and resurrection. Luke says that seven demons had gone out of her and that she helped support the group from her resources. Her name is usually taken to mean that she came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdala">Magdala</a>, a village on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a>. In the gospel of John she is the first to see the risen Jesus and is sent to tell the apostles, so some traditions call her the apostle to the apostles. A Western tradition, shaped by a sermon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a> in 591, made her a repentant prostitute, which has no basis in the gospels.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mary-magdalene.jpg" alt="Mary Magdalene" width="330" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sibeaster · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Noli_me_tangere_(Monreale).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Women in most Gnostic literature are portrayed as chaotic and disobedient, while the Nag Hammadi texts place women in roles of leadership and heroism, and some texts, such as the Gospel of Mary, treat <b>Mary Magdalene</b> as a leader superior to the twelve apostles.</blockquote><a href="/religion/gnosticism">Gnosticism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In the edit a line naming Mary was changed, because modern scholarship no longer identifies <b>Mary Magdalene</b> with the woman taken in adultery.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>The largest recorded flood in central Europe struck on and around the feast day of St. <b>Mary Magdalene</b>, and became known by her name.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-22">22 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
